6. The component itself remains unchanged, we're just providing the necessary context values it needs

This approach should work better because:
1. We're not trying to modify read-only module properties
2. We're using React's built-in context system
3. The mock values are properly scoped to the component
4. All the necessary data is still provided to the component

The component will render exactly as it does in the main application, just with our mock data provided through React context instead of the actual hooks.

